            <ArticleTitle>Mean-Field Scaling of the Superfluid to Mott Insulator Transition in a 2D Optical Superlattice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>100402</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1103/PhysRevLett.119.100402</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The mean-field treatment of the Bose-Hubbard model predicts properties of lattice-trapped gases to be insensitive to the specific lattice geometry once system energies are scaled by the lattice coordination number z. We test this scaling directly by comparing coherence properties of ^{87}Rb gases that are driven across the superfluid to Mott insulator transition within optical lattices of either the kagome (z=4) or the triangular (z=6) geometries. The coherent fraction measured for atoms in the kagome lattice is lower than for those in a triangular lattice with the same interaction and tunneling energies. A comparison of measurements from both lattices agrees quantitatively with the scaling prediction. We also study the response of the gas to a change in lattice geometry, and observe the dynamics as a strongly interacting kagome-lattice gas is suddenly &quot;hole doped&quot; by introducing the additional sites of the triangular lattice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
